## Solution

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains no leaked solution, proof sketch, or answer value. Proceeding.

---

Let $A=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}$ with integer entries, and let $\Phi:\mathbb T^2\to\mathbb T^2$ be the induced map $\Phi([x,y])=[ax+by,\,cx+dy]$.

**Well-definedness.** Since $a,b,c,d\in\mathbb Z$, the linear map $A:\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R^2$ sends $\mathbb Z^2$ into $\mathbb Z^2$, so $\Phi$ descends to a well-defined smooth map on $\mathbb T^2=\mathbb R^2/\mathbb Z^2$.

We compute $\deg\Phi$ by two complementary arguments.

---

### Argument 1 (homology / exterior algebra)

The torus has $H_1(\mathbb T^2;\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z^2$ with standard generators $e_1,e_2$ (the two coordinate loops), and
$$H_2(\mathbb T^2;\mathbb Z)\cong \bigwedge\nolimits^2 H_1(\mathbb T^2;\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z,$$
with generator $e_1\wedge e_2$ corresponding to the fundamental class $[\mathbb T^2]$ via the Künneth / universal coefficient identification.

The induced map $\Phi_*$ on $H_1$ is exactly the matrix $A$: the loop $t\mapsto[t,0]$ maps to $t\mapsto[at,ct]$, giving the first column $(a,c)$, and similarly the second loop gives column $(b,d)$. Concretely $\Phi_*(e_1)=a\,e_1+c\,e_2$ and $\Phi_*(e_2)=b\,e_1+d\,e_2$.

By naturality of the exterior-power identification,
$$\Phi_*(e_1\wedge e_2)=\Phi_*(e_1)\wedge\Phi_*(e_2)=(a\,e_1+c\,e_2)\wedge(b\,e_1+d\,e_2)=(ad-bc)\,e_1\wedge e_2.$$

Since $e_1\wedge e_2$ represents $[\mathbb T^2]$, the map $\Phi_*:H_2(\mathbb T^2)\to H_2(\mathbb T^2)$ is multiplication by $\det A=ad-bc$. By definition of degree for a map between compact oriented manifolds of the same dimension,
$$\boxed{\deg\Phi=\det A.}$$

---

### Argument 2 (regular value / local degree) — confirms the sign

**Case $\det A\neq 0$.** Choose a regular value $[p]\in\mathbb T^2$ (almost every point is regular). The preimage consists of classes $[x]$ with $Ax\equiv p\pmod{\mathbb Z^2}$, i.e.
$$x=A^{-1}(p+n),\qquad n\in\mathbb Z^2.$$
Two solutions $A^{-1}(p+n)$ and $A^{-1}(p+m)$ give the same class in $\mathbb T^2$ iff $A^{-1}(n-m)\in\mathbb Z^2$, i.e. $n-m\in A(\mathbb Z^2)$. Hence the number of distinct preimages equals the index
$$\bigl[\mathbb Z^2:A(\mathbb Z^2)\bigr]=|\det A|.$$
At every preimage the Jacobian of $\Phi$ (in the standard flat coordinates) is exactly $A$, so every local degree equals $\operatorname{sgn}(\det A)$. Summing,
$$\deg\Phi=|\det A|\cdot\operatorname{sgn}(\det A)=\det A.$$

**Case $\det A=0$.** Then $\operatorname{rank}A\le 1$, so the image of $A$ is contained in a line (or point) in $\mathbb R^2$; consequently $\Phi(\mathbb T^2)$ lies in a $1$-dimensional (or $0$-dimensional) subtorus of $\mathbb T^2$, which has empty interior. A regular value outside this image has empty preimage, so
$$\deg\Phi=0=\det A.$$

---

Both arguments agree: **$\deg\Phi=\det A$** in all cases. The homology argument explains *why* the determinant appears — the degree is the action of $\Phi$ on $H_2\cong\bigwedge^2 H_1$, and the action on $\bigwedge^2\mathbb Z^2$ of the linear map $A$ on $H_1\cong\mathbb Z^2$ is precisely $\det A$.

